1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,340
Reach and lift and hoist and haul.

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:07,780
Up and down and across and over.

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,380
Nothing puts in a workout like a crane.

4
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:18,700
They keep in shape by building skyscrapers, loading ships, making movies, even reaching into space.

5
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:20,300
And it pays off, too.

6
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,740
They're sleek, they're strong, and they're huge.

7
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:28,680
Now, Cranes on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Imagine if you could extend your arm to twice its length.

9
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:50,560
No, wait.

10
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:52,540
Make that 100 times its length.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,020
Now imagine that it could lift three or maybe 800 times more than it can lift now.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,429
That'd be something.

13
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:07,000
Actually, that would be a crane, one of the most useful machines ever created.

14
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:13,000
You see cranes in every facet of today's society, particularly when we're building.

15
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:18,000
They're used for marine work. We see cranes on barges.

16
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:24,000
We see cranes on the shore to load and unload cargoes off of ships.

17
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:28,580
We see cranes on railroad yards to unload the cargo there.

18
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:32,220
We see cranes on practically every construction job.

19
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:39,340
It's like playing a big Donkey Kong game, trying to control that hook and steady the hook.

20
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:44,800
It's like everything else. As long as it keeps looking simple, we're fine.

21
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:49,000
The complexity involved in a crane is unbelievable.

22
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:52,260
Cranes in a shop like this, you have to have them.

23
00:01:52,261 --> 00:01:58,660
I cannot envision even trying to do business or do things with the number of people we have here without them.

24
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:04,300
Cranes are hoisting machines that are capable of lifting loads vertically and horizontally as well.

25
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:09,100
Some cranes even pivot around in what's called a slewing motion.

26
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:13,200
And they come in all shapes, sizes, and types.

27
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,920
From little forklifts to giant derricks.

28
00:02:16,921 --> 00:02:23,300
In between, you'll find gantry cranes, bridge cranes, even mobile cranes.

29
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:27,500
The biggest crane of all? The tower crane.

30
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:33,040
This is a Piener SK-575 crane manufactured in West Germany.

31
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:37,720
All the features that you see that are on the crane right now are state-of-the-art.

32
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:43,260
The maximum load for this crane is 29,000 pounds,

33
00:02:43,261 --> 00:02:51,960
and all of this comes together in one point, 15 by 15 at the base of the crane and about 12 feet deep.

34
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:56,060
Tower cranes are often planted into the ground in a cement foundation

35
00:02:56,061 --> 00:03:02,580
that can weigh as much as 400,000 pounds and provides a solid anchorage for the structure.

36
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:07,600
When you do need a tower crane, it's because you need it 100% of the time.

37
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:11,300
It goes in, it's one of the very first foundations that you put in.

38
00:03:11,301 --> 00:03:12,001
J.A.

39
00:03:12,002 --> 00:03:17,420
Jones Construction is putting up a building on a challenging Santa Monica, California job site.

40
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:20,380
To the north is a freeway.

41
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:23,560
To the south, a police station.

42
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:26,040
East and west are crowded streets.

43
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:35,660
With a reach of more than 260 feet, this crane extends over the busy roads as it lifts material on its metal hook.

44
00:03:35,661 --> 00:03:44,960
But this crane's high-tech electronics will shut it down if it tries to lift something beyond its load capacity.

45
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:48,380
Or if the load begins to sway too much.

46
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:54,200
Further, it can absorb tremors from earthquakes, a real danger here.

47
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:58,140
Wind speeds over 30 miles an hour will shut it down.

48
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:03,720
But it can withstand hurricane-force gales by going into weather vane mode.

49
00:04:03,721 --> 00:04:07,060
allowing the jib to release and swing in the wind.

50
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:11,100
And given its proximity to the Pacific Ocean,

51
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:18,460
this 150-ton, 110-foot-tall crane is built to resist salt and wind.

52
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:22,900
As you can see, we have a captain's chair, and I have two joysticks.

53
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:26,300
Right-hand joysticks run the hoist up and down.

54
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:32,120
Left-hand joysticks here run the trolley in and trolley out.

55
00:04:33,720 --> 00:04:36,000
Tower cranes are simply big cantilevers.

56
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,560
A cantilever is a structure that extends from a single support.

57
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:46,280
The vertical mast of a tower crane anchors the horizontal boom, also known as a jib arm.

58
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,680
At the end of the jib, a pulley is used for hoisting.

59
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:56,480
Often the pulley can slide along the jib on a trolley as the jib pivots on the mast.

60
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:07,820
The jib pivots because of a slewing unit, which incorporates the jib, a machinery arm, lots of motors and electrical gear, plus the cab where the operator works.

61
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:14,480
Finally, counterweights at the opposite end of the jib act as an opposing force to the jib's load.

62
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:16,580
It's that simple.

63
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:19,400
Only, it's not that simple at all.

64
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:21,720
In fact, the controls are quite complex.

65
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:26,040
It's all computerized. There's levers. There's switches.

66
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:30,520
The crane operator has to be trained to operate those things.

67
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:34,500
It's a matter of understanding the computer program.

68
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:39,720
This is our computer right now, our screen digital readout.

69
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:46,000
It can read to me the weight chart, the radius of the trolley, how far out the distance I'm at.

70
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:50,900
It reads the capacity of the crane, the wind speed factor, and the percentage of my weight capacity.

71
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:55,860
The computer and circuitry on this crane is almost as large as the cab.

72
00:05:56,480 --> 00:05:59,700
This is our main computer panel right here, the A panel.

73
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:05,420
It reads out the trolley, the swing motors, and our main computer, the brain of the crane itself.

74
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:09,480
As you can see, pretty high-tech stuff.

75
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:13,420
Plus, it speaks German and French, in addition to English.

76
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:20,400
Despite their size and complexity, cranes like this tower crane can be assembled and disassembled quickly.

77
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:26,180
When the job's done, they're trucked to another site to be built anew.

78
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:30,840
With the help of a portable crane on the back of a truck or tractor,

79
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:37,180
the tower crane is constructed out of interconnected bars of steel, much like an erector set.

80
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:43,280
Finally, the slewing unit is hoisted onto the mast.

81
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:46,520
The whole process may take only a couple of days.

82
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:49,880
The crane has open web members.

83
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:52,960
Another name for it is lattice.

84
00:06:52,961 --> 00:07:00,400
and that's really the structural guts of the crane that derives its strength.

85
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,820
So you don't need to box it in and you don't need to cover it.

86
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:08,820
As long as you have your structural skeleton to make it work, that's all you really need.

87
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:14,040
Tower cranes are assembled in sections when they help build very tall skyscrapers.

88
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:21,920
They employ a hydraulic jacking device to hoist the jib, cab, and climbing unit up from the top of the mast.

89
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:27,140
so that a new section of the crane mast can then be inserted underneath them.

90
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:31,920
Theoretically, a crane could keep building indefinitely,

91
00:07:32,820 --> 00:07:35,820
although strength and leverage decrease as height increases.

92
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:42,340
And when you're teetering on a 250-foot pile of steel toothpicks, leverage is important.

93
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:47,760
Actually, if you have motion sickness and you're afraid of heights, you wouldn't belong in the sea.

94
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:50,180
Cranes are hoisting maniacs.

95
00:07:50,181 --> 00:07:54,460
They literally pull an office building or a skyscraper up from its foundation

96
00:07:54,461 --> 00:07:58,700
as girders and partitions are lifted and positioned with the greatest of ease.

97
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:04,560
Well, this one loads everything into the building, moves all the formwork,

98
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,440
brings up all of the reinforcing steel for the concrete,

99
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:10,040
takes the forms from one floor to the other,

100
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:14,000
brings in all of the other supplies for all the other subcontractors.

101
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:17,560
This crane and all the others like it do something else.

102
00:08:18,340 --> 00:08:19,520
They lift the economy.

103
00:08:19,521 --> 00:08:23,440
It's hard to quantify the impact of cranes on our economy.

104
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:29,240
The construction industry represents about 10% of America's gross national product.

105
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:35,360
And you cannot build without cranes, so it's a very vital part.

106
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:40,380
Cranes have been helping our civilization move forward ever since we've had a civilization.

107
00:08:41,380 --> 00:08:43,220
Up next, ancient cranes.

108
00:08:43,221 --> 00:08:50,580
how early engineers may have used cranes to build the pyramids, the Colosseum, and the great cathedrals.

109
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:59,140
Contractors can expect to pay as much as $20,000 per month in rental fees for a tower crane.

110
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:01,520
Cranes will return on Modern Marvels.

111
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:08,020
In Manitowoc, Wisconsin, they make cranes.

112
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:10,760
Big cranes. The travel.

113
00:09:10,761 --> 00:09:18,320
At over 640 feet high, their largest models rival some stationary tower cranes and their

114
00:09:18,321 --> 00:09:20,820
ability to lift heavy objects high off the ground.

115
00:09:23,260 --> 00:09:26,020
But with portability, they're in many ways more valuable.

116
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:29,720
They even perform some of the same tasks.

117
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:32,980
For that matter, they even assemble tower cranes.

118
00:09:35,540 --> 00:09:37,880
And they're designed and built right here.

119
00:09:37,881 --> 00:09:47,120
engineers pretty much are the designers of our cranes there are several people it can be anywhere

120
00:09:47,121 --> 00:09:51,300
from customers to sale people that come up with the ideas really it's in-house engineering that

121
00:09:51,301 --> 00:09:57,280
comes up with the designs Manitowoc makes different types of portable cranes from

122
00:09:57,281 --> 00:10:00,840
telescopic crawler cranes that can extend their jibs hydraulically

123
00:10:00,841 --> 00:10:10,400
to boom trucks, small cranes that can travel the roadways and which sport modest telescopic

124
00:10:10,401 --> 00:10:16,360
booms. After the designs are approved, the next stage is manufactured.

125
00:10:16,361 --> 00:10:20,518
Really it's somewhat quite simple. It starts on the far end of the facility.

126
00:10:20,519 --> 00:10:21,219
It comes in

127
00:10:21,220 --> 00:10:24,653
as raw steel. We cut, we burn on the plasma burn table.

128
00:10:24,654 --> 00:10:26,580
We have large weldments, small

129
00:10:26,581 --> 00:10:31,260
weldments that we make all comes together in the assembly area. Once it's assembled,

130
00:10:31,261 --> 00:10:34,720
it comes out here to the test yard, and from there we knock it down, put it on trucks,

131
00:10:34,721 --> 00:10:39,282
and ship it to the customer. The advantages of a mobile crane are obvious.

132
00:10:39,283 --> 00:10:39,983
They can go

133
00:10:39,984 --> 00:10:43,256
from job to job with a minimum of site preparation.

134
00:10:43,257 --> 00:10:46,100
With their jibs and mast all folded up, they

135
00:10:46,101 --> 00:10:47,793
can transport themselves.

136
00:10:47,794 --> 00:10:52,600
And by deploying outriggers from their chassis to create a larger base,

137
00:10:52,601 --> 00:10:54,383
can achieve suitable stability.

138
00:10:54,384 --> 00:10:57,660
And all you have to do to pull up the outriggers is press

139
00:10:57,661 --> 00:11:04,000
a button. How beautiful is that? Maybe beautiful is the wrong word.

140
00:11:04,001 --> 00:11:07,378
Cranes aren't designed aesthetically. They're a working tool.

141
00:11:07,379 --> 00:11:09,040
You think of them like a mule.

142
00:11:09,041 --> 00:11:10,210
They're there to do work.

143
00:11:10,211 --> 00:11:13,720
They're not an architectural feature of anything because they're temporary.

144
00:11:13,721 --> 00:11:15,147
They're not going to stay there.

145
00:11:15,148 --> 00:11:17,600
So the only time you're going to see them is during the

146
00:11:17,601 --> 00:11:23,400
transitional period of construction. True, they're a little awkward looking, but for

147
00:11:23,401 --> 00:11:27,220
something still in its awkward stage, the crane has been around for an awfully long

148
00:11:27,221 --> 00:11:31,738
time. In fact, no one really knows when they were first used.

149
00:11:31,739 --> 00:11:33,960
But they were almost certainly

150
00:11:33,961 --> 00:11:41,680
employed when man began using large stones for construction, roughly 4,500 years ago.

151
00:11:41,681 --> 00:11:49,360
For example, to move the 5,000-pound stones that make up the Pyramids of Giza.

152
00:11:49,361 --> 00:11:53,780
Early cranes used pulleys.

153
00:11:53,781 --> 00:11:59,720
A pulley is a wheel, sometimes grooved, that employs a rope to convey energy.

154
00:11:59,721 --> 00:12:04,240
When a pulley is connected by an axle to a frame or block, which in turn is connected

155
00:12:04,241 --> 00:12:08,800
by a rope to other pulleys, it creates a block and tackle.

156
00:12:08,801 --> 00:12:11,560
Now you have mechanical advantage.

157
00:12:11,561 --> 00:12:17,060
Mechanical advantage results when a fixed amount of force, for example the force exerted by a single man,

158
00:12:18,060 --> 00:12:22,560
is increased many times through the application of simple mechanisms like the pulley.

159
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:27,060
It all depends on a simple looking physics formula.

160
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:29,560
Work equals force times distance.

161
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:34,060
In this case, rope is used to move a 200 pound object 2 feet.

162
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:39,060
Employing a pulley, the effort exerted by the man is only 100 pounds.

163
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:42,420
But the distance pulled is four feet of rope.

164
00:12:43,020 --> 00:12:46,040
The more pulleys, the greater the mechanical advantage.

165
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:48,440
Cranes had a very slow evolution.

166
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:51,700
It's hard for us to trace it because there was really no documents of it.

167
00:12:51,740 --> 00:12:55,460
And, of course, there was no pictures other than maybe some paintings.

168
00:12:55,680 --> 00:12:58,200
And cranes weren't a favorite topic of painters.

169
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:03,180
Sometimes the best evidence of an ancient crane is the end result of its effort.

170
00:13:03,181 --> 00:13:08,860
Certainly the Hanging Gardens of Babylon and the Colossus of Rhodes could not have been

171
00:13:08,861 --> 00:13:12,260
built without some sort of crane.

172
00:13:12,261 --> 00:13:18,460
But clues like this image of a crane being used to construct the Colosseum in Rome, found

173
00:13:18,461 --> 00:13:23,580
on the tombstone of one of the contractors, explicitly show that cranes were employed

174
00:13:23,581 --> 00:13:26,840
by the ancients.

175
00:13:26,841 --> 00:13:32,980
Wooden cranes were used during the Middle Ages to help build Europe's massive cathedrals.

176
00:13:32,981 --> 00:13:36,500
Treadmills were used as engines, generating the energy to power the cranes.

177
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:42,740
It's interesting that they used human beings inside of a, like a big barrel.

178
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:50,320
And that turned the wheel, which wound the rope, and lifted the load.

179
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:57,780
During the later Middle Ages, early tower cranes like this one were being designed by Leonardo da Vinci.

180
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:02,400
This particular example may even be the first mobile crane ever built.

181
00:14:02,980 --> 00:14:09,740
By the mid-17th century, wooden cranes were a familiar sight at construction projects,

182
00:14:09,741 --> 00:14:13,400
such as this one in Paris, building the Royal Bridge.

183
00:14:13,401 --> 00:14:19,020
But during the 19th century, a new building material would revolutionize the role of the

184
00:14:19,021 --> 00:14:20,020
crane.

185
00:14:20,021 --> 00:14:25,880
With the advent of mass-produced steel in the late 1800s, that just opened the door

186
00:14:25,881 --> 00:14:28,780
for cranes, because we found a new building material.

187
00:14:28,781 --> 00:14:33,320
And with that, we were able to increase our structures a great deal.

188
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:34,600
We took a quantum leap.

189
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:40,300
Steel allowed engineers to reach new heights, both artistic and altitudinal.

190
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:42,680
Steel structures like the Eiffel Tower,

191
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:48,880
the Statue of Liberty,

192
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:53,420
and the hundreds of turn-of-the-century skyscrapers

193
00:14:53,421 --> 00:14:55,940
were dependent on new modern cranes,

194
00:14:55,941 --> 00:15:00,480
which were also made of steel and thus exceptionally strong for their weight.

195
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:03,900
Although early cranes were steam-powered,

196
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:07,180
ultimately they would employ the internal combustion engine.

197
00:15:09,240 --> 00:15:11,940
Companies familiar with structural steel construction

198
00:15:11,941 --> 00:15:14,640
and companies experienced with heavy-duty equipment

199
00:15:14,641 --> 00:15:17,900
began offering cranes for large-scale projects.

200
00:15:18,760 --> 00:15:20,900
As architects continued to make bigger structures,

201
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:24,100
crane manufacturers joined them in the race for the sky.

202
00:15:24,101 --> 00:15:30,040
since the skeleton-framed cranes can easily be built up by simply adding more sections.

203
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:34,660
Today, companies can make cranes to whatever height their clients need,

204
00:15:35,320 --> 00:15:37,200
some of top 35 stories.

205
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:42,780
Nearly every iconic architectural and public works project

206
00:15:42,781 --> 00:15:47,760
from the Empire State Building to the Hoover Dam has relied on cranes.

207
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:52,660
Cranes are easily the most important piece of large equipment on any job,

208
00:15:52,661 --> 00:15:55,520
and they can be one of the most complex.

209
00:15:56,140 --> 00:16:00,600
For example, you may have a 300-ton capacity mobile crane,

210
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:04,340
but that doesn't mean that it will lift 300 tons anywhere it will reach.

211
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:08,300
And so you have stability limitations,

212
00:16:08,301 --> 00:16:14,860
and that would mean that the crane would be lifting the maximum capacity with the boom fairly vertical.

213
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:21,220
As you begin to boom down or to extend the reach, what we call the radius,

214
00:16:21,221 --> 00:16:25,100
then you're not able to lift near as much.

215
00:16:25,101 --> 00:16:29,180
A crane operator needs to know not just what his maximum weight limit is,

216
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:33,860
but where and what that maximum is at any given point in the arc of his lift.

217
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:39,060
If he doesn't, he could seriously injure himself and his co-workers.

218
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:43,620
You're defying the law of gravity and you're doing it in close proximity to people.

219
00:16:43,760 --> 00:16:45,720
So anytime that you do those things,

220
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:50,160
then there are a number of bases that have to be covered for the lift to go safely.

221
00:16:51,020 --> 00:16:54,240
And many times those bases aren't covered,

222
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:57,200
and you have some pretty serious accidents,

223
00:16:57,380 --> 00:16:58,880
and many times they're very catastrophic.

224
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:04,800
Nearly every serious crane accident ultimately can be traced back to human error.

225
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:09,300
Often, the end result is a crippled crane.

226
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:12,040
Occasionally, lives are lost.

227
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:19,040
One of the most serious modern crane accidents was in San Francisco in 1989.

228
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:27,160
Five people were killed when a crane fell 16 stories.

229
00:17:27,161 --> 00:17:36,160
Next, some of the largest cranes ever built never lift anything heavier than a svelte 30-ton container.

230
00:17:36,161 --> 00:17:45,160
A crane operator can make between $60,000 and $100,000 per year, depending on seniority and location.

231
00:17:45,161 --> 00:17:48,160
Overtime can push the number even higher.

232
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:50,820
Cranes will return on Modern Marvels.

233
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:57,660
Looming like steel dinosaurs along the waterfront of major port cities,

234
00:17:58,280 --> 00:18:01,340
giant container cranes are the workhorses of the crane world.

235
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:08,580
These 200-foot-long, 350-foot-high, 22-story cranes

236
00:18:08,581 --> 00:18:11,820
stand at the pier waiting for their ship to come in.

237
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:17,260
But they don't wait for long and they don't stand still

238
00:18:17,261 --> 00:18:19,880
because these mega cranes can roller skate.

239
00:18:20,980 --> 00:18:24,760
A container crane will move up and down the dock.

240
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:26,300
That's the gantry motion.

241
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:29,900
It will move in and out towards the ship and away from the ship.

242
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:31,540
That's trolley motion.

243
00:18:31,541 --> 00:18:34,600
And it'll hoist up and down. That's hoist motion.

244
00:18:35,420 --> 00:18:37,760
So we call them a gantry crane because they gantry.

245
00:18:38,660 --> 00:18:41,880
Rolling on wheels along rails parallel to the docks,

246
00:18:42,520 --> 00:18:45,880
gantry container cranes weighing 1,320 tons

247
00:18:45,881 --> 00:18:49,940
have revolutionized the shipping industry with their efficiency.

248
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:55,780
Each ship is divided into 50-foot sections,

249
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:59,480
with a few cranes loading or unloading at the same time,

250
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:04,100
each crane working a section while discharging 30 containers an hour.

251
00:19:05,400 --> 00:19:12,340
To offload a ship today, we can do it probably in eight hours with a container crane,

252
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:15,040
whereas it used to take maybe three weeks before.

253
00:19:15,041 --> 00:19:24,120
As our ships get bigger and bigger, our cranes have to get bigger and bigger to lift these container loads off the ships and also to load them.

254
00:19:24,820 --> 00:19:30,340
And so the cranes that we were using 12, 15 years ago are obsolete. We can't use it.

255
00:19:31,660 --> 00:19:39,400
In the year 2000, the Port of Oakland, California received the first four of eight brand new container cranes,

256
00:19:39,560 --> 00:19:44,960
made to order and shipped fully constructed on a special ship from Shanghai, China.

257
00:19:45,040 --> 00:19:48,280
Price tag? About $7 million a piece.

258
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:54,000
The trick was getting them to fit under the Golden Gate and the even lower Bay Bridge.

259
00:19:54,680 --> 00:20:00,980
Obviously, as the cranes get higher, you want the height of the crane to be higher to control the boom lift.

260
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:08,760
And so we designed those cranes to be within a meter of the bridge at low tide.

261
00:20:09,140 --> 00:20:11,720
So we had to be really careful when we brought them in.

262
00:20:11,721 --> 00:20:20,340
The ship is designed to ballast up and down during the offloading to maintain the height of the ship during tidal changes,

263
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:25,440
so we could actually ballast the ship right down to the water level when it came under the bridge.

264
00:20:28,280 --> 00:20:36,300
Because they're unloaded, fully assembled, the cranes like these at Oakland are usually up, tested, and working within 15 days of arrival,

265
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:40,380
which is good because ports like Oakland are always busy.

266
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:45,840
with an average of five to seven massive ships loading and unloading each day.

267
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:51,920
Electrical engineer Wan Yong, who works for ZPMC,

268
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:54,040
the company that built the cranes,

269
00:20:54,720 --> 00:20:56,960
helps ensure that the cranes are working properly.

270
00:21:00,520 --> 00:21:04,680
Wan Yong gives the cranes a quick test drive before they go operational.

271
00:21:11,720 --> 00:21:18,720
He helped design and install the systems that coordinate the 20 computers on this crane.

272
00:21:18,721 --> 00:21:25,720
Each crane has its own 12,000-volt substation housed in a room way up inside.

273
00:21:25,721 --> 00:21:31,720
The computers each track a different function, such as air conditioning or the elevator.

274
00:21:31,721 --> 00:21:36,720
But the main computer operates the hoist, trolley and gantry moves.

275
00:21:36,721 --> 00:21:40,740
Unlike on a tower crane, the cab on a container crane does some traveling.

276
00:21:41,780 --> 00:21:44,560
Two joysticks operate the hoist and the trolley.

277
00:21:45,740 --> 00:21:49,140
Another computer keeps the cranes from gantry-ing into each other.

278
00:21:49,780 --> 00:21:54,280
An interesting thing about a container crane is that it's a very specific design.

279
00:21:54,700 --> 00:21:56,020
All it does is move containers.

280
00:21:56,860 --> 00:21:58,500
Handles cargo on and off the ships.

281
00:21:58,501 --> 00:22:00,900
They don't do heavy loads.

282
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:02,960
They don't do a lot of unusual things.

283
00:22:03,380 --> 00:22:05,700
But they do move containers very fast.

284
00:22:06,720 --> 00:22:11,720
The cranes are designed around standard container sizes, usually 40 feet by 8 feet.

285
00:22:12,780 --> 00:22:16,900
Inside each container are 20 to 30 tons of goods to be shipped or unloaded,

286
00:22:17,400 --> 00:22:19,520
possibly onto a truck or a rail car.

287
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:25,200
A coupling device in the crane hoist connects with the four corners of the container,

288
00:22:26,320 --> 00:22:31,080
hooks it, then hoists and swings it up and away,

289
00:22:31,920 --> 00:22:35,480
as high as 140 feet above the dock.

290
00:22:36,720 --> 00:22:44,700
Using containers to ship things may seem obvious, but they've only become the industry standard since their first application in the 1960s.

291
00:22:46,040 --> 00:22:49,820
Early dockside cranes didn't need to be nearly as strong or tall.

292
00:22:49,960 --> 00:22:51,562
Every box sat on a pallet.

293
00:22:51,563 --> 00:22:59,820
We reached down with a crane, lifted up the pallet, took it off, set it on the dock, and then moved the pallet around with a forklift.

294
00:23:00,320 --> 00:23:05,000
The first containers were built here, and the first container cranes were made here.

295
00:23:05,001 --> 00:23:11,060
we designed the crane and the containers and the ships to handle the containers all at the same time.

296
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:12,720
They were designed together.

297
00:23:14,340 --> 00:23:18,020
Now the whole process works like some sort of blue-collar ballet

298
00:23:18,021 --> 00:23:24,220
as the containers lift and swing and drop off the trucks and onto the ships.

299
00:23:24,221 --> 00:23:27,180
You don't realize it because they make it look pretty simple,

300
00:23:27,340 --> 00:23:29,940
but if you get up there and try and operate it yourself,

301
00:23:29,941 --> 00:23:37,660
you'll see that a 40-ton container hanging from a set of wires that's 100 feet long is a big pendulum.

302
00:23:38,200 --> 00:23:40,020
And you have to be able to control that.

303
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:41,480
That's not easy to do.

304
00:23:42,640 --> 00:23:47,640
Not all waterfront cranes are giant container cranes maneuvering to load big ships.

305
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:51,280
Some, like this railway gantry, are more construction-oriented.

306
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:57,260
And these seasoned veterans actually help build ships, towboats, and barges

307
00:23:57,261 --> 00:24:00,260
in Jeffersonville, Indiana, along the Ohio River.

308
00:24:01,740 --> 00:24:07,160
Well, the shipyard was established in the 1830s as the Howard Shipyard

309
00:24:07,161 --> 00:24:10,840
and later became Jeff Boat in the late 30s.

310
00:24:11,280 --> 00:24:13,960
And at that time, the cranes were put in place by the Navy.

311
00:24:14,580 --> 00:24:17,400
The originals now have been replaced by the ones we have now,

312
00:24:17,960 --> 00:24:21,640
which are 1960, 1970 models.

313
00:24:22,560 --> 00:24:26,740
Weighing in at about 300 tons and topping out at 95 feet,

314
00:24:26,741 --> 00:24:30,980
These gantry cranes are put to all-purpose use here in this shipyard,

315
00:24:31,300 --> 00:24:35,620
moving unwieldy materials and building ships.

316
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:39,520
Sometimes they have to work as a team.

317
00:24:40,740 --> 00:24:45,240
We use a two-crane lift any time that we get anything that's heavier than 55 tons.

318
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:51,320
And we do that with our side boxes, our bows, and anything that's above 55 tons.

319
00:24:51,760 --> 00:24:53,080
These are very complicated.

320
00:24:53,081 --> 00:24:55,360
The cranes, you must keep the load level.

321
00:24:55,361 --> 00:25:00,060
The cranes must work in unison so that you're not putting strain on one and not the other,

322
00:25:00,420 --> 00:25:01,980
and you must keep the load balanced.

323
00:25:04,100 --> 00:25:06,880
These cranes aren't the only cranes working the waterfront.

324
00:25:07,780 --> 00:25:11,020
Jeff Boat has access to a floating crane mounted on a barge.

325
00:25:11,680 --> 00:25:14,320
Good for those hard-to-reach places just offshore.

326
00:25:15,600 --> 00:25:18,020
The barge crane we have here is a Clyde.

327
00:25:18,060 --> 00:25:19,400
It was built in 1949.

328
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:22,860
We mounted it on a barge probably in the 70s.

329
00:25:22,861 --> 00:25:25,060
It has a capacity of 30 tons.

330
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:30,700
We use it basically for re-taking out engines, replacing engines, gearboxes, and towbooks.

331
00:25:31,880 --> 00:25:34,720
Next, they can climb up skyscrapers.

332
00:25:35,020 --> 00:25:36,360
They can skate next to ships.

333
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:38,440
Is there any place a crane can't go?

334
00:25:42,160 --> 00:25:45,880
The Port of Oakland, California, the fourth busiest port in the nation,

335
00:25:46,380 --> 00:25:51,160
has over 31 gantry cranes working more than 19 miles of waterfront.

336
00:25:51,161 --> 00:25:54,740
Cranes will return on Modern Marvels.

337
00:25:56,680 --> 00:25:57,420
Here's one.

338
00:25:58,360 --> 00:25:59,380
There's another one.

339
00:26:00,300 --> 00:26:02,420
That right there, it's a crane too.

340
00:26:03,860 --> 00:26:07,460
Cranes have so ingratiated themselves into our world that once you know where to look,

341
00:26:08,540 --> 00:26:10,280
you can't help seeing them.

342
00:26:10,840 --> 00:26:15,280
When you think about it, everything that's made has to be lifted and moved at one time or the other,

343
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:18,640
and cranes play a big part in doing that.

344
00:26:18,641 --> 00:26:24,640
For instance, driving down the road, that long arm sticking off the back of that tow truck?

345
00:26:24,641 --> 00:26:29,640
It's a crane. Sort of a miniature version of the mobile construction crane.

346
00:26:29,641 --> 00:26:36,640
Yet these hydraulically powered derricks are capable of picking up any vehicles certified to travel the roadways,

347
00:26:36,641 --> 00:26:39,640
including big rigs weighing over 70 tons.

348
00:26:39,641 --> 00:26:46,640
Using portable remote controls, these trucks can lift while the operator stands a safe distance away.

349
00:26:46,641 --> 00:26:52,180
Like their mobile crane cousins, tow vehicles use outriggers to brace against the ground.

350
00:26:52,980 --> 00:26:56,560
And they have booms with complete 360-degree mobility.

351
00:26:57,560 --> 00:27:05,520
The crane is such a vital element of the auto-towing industry that it was a key component of the very first tow truck ever built.

352
00:27:06,380 --> 00:27:09,940
A twin boom crane mounted on the back of an old Cadillac.

353
00:27:09,941 --> 00:27:14,900
Even before the tow truck made its first appearance,

354
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:19,460
railroads solved the problem of derailments with a similar steam-powered crane

355
00:27:19,461 --> 00:27:22,040
that ran independently along the rails.

356
00:27:22,460 --> 00:27:24,200
When you had a car that came off the tracks,

357
00:27:24,340 --> 00:27:26,340
generally the railroad would bring in their derrick.

358
00:27:26,740 --> 00:27:27,700
We call it the big hook.

359
00:27:28,120 --> 00:27:30,940
And they would use that to lift the cars back up onto the tracks.

360
00:27:30,941 --> 00:27:32,860
Our derrick was built in 1917.

361
00:27:33,220 --> 00:27:37,000
It was modified in the 1970s to diesel electric power.

362
00:27:37,460 --> 00:27:39,840
And it has 150-ton lifting capacity.

363
00:27:39,940 --> 00:27:46,940
These specialized railroad cranes also move the occasional fallen tree and rolled boulder.

364
00:27:46,941 --> 00:27:51,940
Take the hook off the railroad derrick and replace it with a big scoop.

365
00:27:51,941 --> 00:27:54,940
Then switch out the wheels for tires.

366
00:27:54,941 --> 00:27:59,940
And you've got yourself a construction shovel.

367
00:28:03,940 --> 00:28:07,940
Remove the tracks off the shovel, stick the crane on a barge,

368
00:28:07,941 --> 00:28:11,180
And you've got a drag line or dredge.

369
00:28:11,181 --> 00:28:16,080
These giant machines help scoop out canal bottoms and maintain ports.

370
00:28:16,081 --> 00:28:24,980
And their floating platforms enable them to reach places only a vessel can go.

371
00:28:24,981 --> 00:28:28,660
Another application is what we call a clamshell.

372
00:28:28,661 --> 00:28:35,900
Which most kids are familiar with it because when they play arcade games, one of the arcade

373
00:28:35,901 --> 00:28:41,400
games is you have this crane with a big clamshell on it to go and pick up a toy.

374
00:28:50,880 --> 00:28:54,200
So a clamshell is, I would classify as a crane also.

375
00:28:54,840 --> 00:28:58,700
For sheer variety, no place beats a warehouse for crane spotting.

376
00:28:59,340 --> 00:29:04,380
Here at Washington Ironworks in Gardena, California, workers use gantry cranes and

377
00:29:04,381 --> 00:29:07,960
forklifts to move large pieces of architectural metal around the shop.

378
00:29:09,440 --> 00:29:13,880
Yep, even a forklift is a crane, because it can lift up and move over.

379
00:29:17,120 --> 00:29:21,880
But look closer. The very walls and ceiling of this shop have cranes.

380
00:29:22,500 --> 00:29:27,620
We have the overhead bridges here, which are five-ton rated service bridges,

381
00:29:27,621 --> 00:29:33,740
one old truss-style bridge, two more modern-family single-span girder bridges.

382
00:29:33,741 --> 00:29:37,540
We have two three-ton overhead trolleys in the architectural shop.

383
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:42,460
We have another five-ton rented bridge just next to here on the stockyard.

384
00:29:42,800 --> 00:29:45,980
I don't even know the count on how many cranes we have here, but there's a lot of them.

385
00:29:46,400 --> 00:29:51,600
These bridge cranes are steel beams which run on rails built into the side of the warehouse walls

386
00:29:51,601 --> 00:29:54,140
and which run the length of the warehouse.

387
00:29:55,100 --> 00:30:00,340
A trolley runs along the beam itself, and a pulley on the trolley can raise or lower a hook.

388
00:30:00,341 --> 00:30:07,340
Workers at the warehouse can manipulate each function of the crane using hand controls.

389
00:30:07,341 --> 00:30:11,340
Without them, working this iron would be nearly impossible.

390
00:30:11,341 --> 00:30:14,340
It's not a luxury. It's a necessity. Just think of it this way.

391
00:30:14,341 --> 00:30:18,340
Three or four people would have to pick up a 250-pound piece.

392
00:30:18,341 --> 00:30:23,340
One man with a crane can pick that up and move that safely and get it to where it has to be.

393
00:30:23,341 --> 00:30:29,020
and you multiply the savings on three or four guys spending 15 minutes moving a piece

394
00:30:29,021 --> 00:30:33,220
as opposed to one man doing seven minutes, the savings are phenomenal.

395
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:39,080
Workers who operate the cranes go through a rigorous training session and weekly update seminars.

396
00:30:39,940 --> 00:30:44,140
The cranes are also built with warning signals to prevent them from crashing into each other.

397
00:30:45,200 --> 00:30:50,040
Plus, they slow down within 10 feet of each other, or at least they're supposed to.

398
00:30:50,041 --> 00:30:54,540
There have been accidents before where that mechanism has failed.

399
00:30:54,880 --> 00:30:58,520
It's never affected the integrity of the crane or the load or ever hurt anybody.

400
00:30:58,860 --> 00:31:00,420
We just had two cranes smashing together.

401
00:31:03,660 --> 00:31:08,680
Cranes are so abundant that even movies and television shows make use of special lightweight ones.

402
00:31:09,680 --> 00:31:13,940
That's because in Hollywood, cranes and cameras go together like sex and violence.

403
00:31:15,020 --> 00:31:17,160
It's lights, camera, cranes.

404
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:18,960
Here on the set of TV's Charmed.

405
00:31:18,961 --> 00:31:29,729
The crane allows us to place the camera up in the air, make swooping moves onto the boat, go above the water, be two or three feet below the dock to see right on the

406
00:31:29,730 --> 00:31:31,100
surface of the water.

407
00:31:31,260 --> 00:31:37,240
Just in general to put the camera in areas where we can make moves that a dolly can't make.

408
00:31:37,860 --> 00:31:44,340
The termed crew is using a lightweight jib made by Aerocrane that won't put too much stress on this rickety dock.

409
00:31:44,341 --> 00:31:49,420
Hollywood has been using cranes to get those big sweeping shots for years.

410
00:31:50,300 --> 00:31:53,960
In the early days, it didn't matter if you used a squeaky old construction crane.

411
00:31:56,280 --> 00:32:00,166
But after the introduction of sound, directors like Cecil B.

412
00:32:00,167 --> 00:32:01,980
DeMille and Alfred Hitchcock

413
00:32:01,981 --> 00:32:06,300
needed quiet, agile, yet sturdy cranes to get that million-dollar shot.

414
00:32:07,420 --> 00:32:11,580
Called cherry pickers, they were modified from cranes developed during World War II

415
00:32:11,581 --> 00:32:14,020
to place bombs in airplanes.

416
00:32:14,340 --> 00:32:20,660
Many of today's movie cranes are often lightweight, portable, and easily assembled.

417
00:32:21,260 --> 00:32:24,600
The aerocrane, as opposed to being a welded box,

418
00:32:24,820 --> 00:32:29,140
welds tend to crack under stress and pressure.

419
00:32:29,600 --> 00:32:30,580
There's extruded aluminum.

420
00:32:30,581 --> 00:32:35,360
You get basically the same strength out of a much smaller profile without welds.

421
00:32:35,680 --> 00:32:38,880
And a smaller profile makes it easier for the crew to carry it around.

422
00:32:39,920 --> 00:32:41,460
This aerocrane can be built by one person.

423
00:32:41,461 --> 00:32:48,640
Today's cranes don't have to be sturdy enough to hold a camera plus a director and a cinematographer.

424
00:32:49,360 --> 00:32:53,200
With omnidirectional remote capabilities and video monitors included,

425
00:32:53,880 --> 00:32:57,880
all you need to do is stick the camera on the end and make the magic happen.

426
00:32:58,780 --> 00:33:02,160
Cranes give the directors much more versatility in filmmaking,

427
00:33:02,920 --> 00:33:04,860
as opposed to just a couple of talking heads.

428
00:33:05,560 --> 00:33:10,560
The director can take the audience from being intimate in a conversation between two people.

429
00:33:10,561 --> 00:33:13,720
pan back, give the audience a view of what they're looking at,

430
00:33:13,840 --> 00:33:15,080
also change their perspective.

431
00:33:15,780 --> 00:33:17,020
They're no longer at the table.

432
00:33:17,360 --> 00:33:19,280
They're a fly on the wall.

433
00:33:20,420 --> 00:33:25,020
Next, cranes go from being a fly on the wall to a bird in the tree

434
00:33:25,021 --> 00:33:29,480
as one group of scientists plant a tower crane in the middle of the woods.

435
00:33:30,040 --> 00:33:33,740
Plus, another group of scientists have launched cranes into space.

436
00:33:35,020 --> 00:33:39,120
At this very moment, one is clinging to the International Space Station.

437
00:33:39,920 --> 00:33:45,280
The largest single-bucket digging machine ever made was over 222 feet tall

438
00:33:45,281 --> 00:33:48,340
and could hold 220 cubic yards of material.

439
00:33:49,380 --> 00:33:54,640
This crane, called Big Muskie, was scrapped in 1999 after 22 years of service.

440
00:33:55,560 --> 00:33:57,420
Cranes will return on Modern Marvels.

441
00:33:59,460 --> 00:34:02,320
Cranes continue to find plenty of use here on Earth

442
00:34:02,321 --> 00:34:06,000
and clearly not just at the familiar construction site or deep water port.

443
00:34:06,001 --> 00:34:12,560
Some are even working amidst the temperate forest greenery of ancient conifer trees,

444
00:34:13,220 --> 00:34:15,380
here in the Pacific Northwest.

445
00:34:16,520 --> 00:34:19,020
It's mostly Douglas fir and western hemlock.

446
00:34:19,240 --> 00:34:22,680
Those are the most dominant trees in this particular stand.

447
00:34:22,980 --> 00:34:25,820
It's kind of a prerequisite to not be afraid of heights.

448
00:34:25,821 --> 00:34:33,500
If you care about global warming and trying to have some influence on that process,

449
00:34:33,501 --> 00:34:40,420
then you obviously care about how places like forests influence that.

450
00:34:41,260 --> 00:34:47,100
Here at the Wind River Research Facility at the Gifford Pinchot National Forest in southwestern Washington state,

451
00:34:48,420 --> 00:34:52,700
scientists use a tower crane to study the canopy of this ancient old-growth forest.

452
00:34:54,100 --> 00:34:57,200
Some of these trees are over 500 years old.

453
00:34:57,201 --> 00:35:02,820
The crane is extraordinary because it allows you to take very heavy equipment

454
00:35:02,821 --> 00:35:05,600
up through the canopy.

455
00:35:05,820 --> 00:35:09,060
You can work anywhere within that three-dimensional space

456
00:35:09,061 --> 00:35:12,240
of the crane, which is about six acres,

457
00:35:12,820 --> 00:35:15,140
that isn't occupied by something solid.

458
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:19,080
And it allows you to take very heavy equipment,

459
00:35:20,020 --> 00:35:24,820
various kinds of sensors and gases and things like that,

460
00:35:25,280 --> 00:35:26,860
into these tree canopies,

461
00:35:26,920 --> 00:35:29,300
which you would not be able to do otherwise.

462
00:35:29,301 --> 00:35:40,160
Erected in 1995, the Wind River Canopy Crane tops out at 250 feet, or 25 stories high.

463
00:35:40,500 --> 00:35:46,620
The only canopy crane in North America, it is also the tallest canopy crane among the half dozen in the world.

464
00:35:47,600 --> 00:35:53,900
It lifts researches as high as 220 feet, allowing them to conduct experiments in the trees.

465
00:35:54,780 --> 00:35:57,000
Winter, summer, rain or shine.

466
00:35:57,001 --> 00:36:03,380
What we're doing today is we're getting an indirect measurement of transpiration,

467
00:36:03,620 --> 00:36:07,480
that is, loss of water vapor from the needles of these conifer trees,

468
00:36:07,680 --> 00:36:09,560
with a device called a porometer.

469
00:36:10,900 --> 00:36:15,780
They are also determining the amount of water tension on columns of water within each tree

470
00:36:15,781 --> 00:36:18,920
as it pulls moisture up like a straw from its roots

471
00:36:18,921 --> 00:36:22,600
and out through pores on those topmost needles.

472
00:36:22,601 --> 00:36:29,160
These scientists are toppling long-held beliefs about old-growth forests.

473
00:36:29,700 --> 00:36:35,900
For instance, these ancient Douglas firs and hemlocks are absorbing more carbon dioxide than a young forest does.

474
00:36:36,620 --> 00:36:40,500
This forest literally breathes, and when the sun comes up in the morning,

475
00:36:40,720 --> 00:36:49,060
there's an initial burp of CO2 that's been moving up through the stand all night long.

476
00:36:49,061 --> 00:36:52,120
Then, of course, all day long it's taking up the CO2.

477
00:36:52,600 --> 00:36:54,600
as photosynthesis kicks in.

478
00:36:57,220 --> 00:37:00,380
One of the great advantages of canopy cranes,

479
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:02,440
at least for tree physiologists like us,

480
00:37:02,920 --> 00:37:06,700
is that they allow us to study trees as whole organisms

481
00:37:06,701 --> 00:37:10,620
and to try to understand how they coordinate and integrate their activities

482
00:37:10,621 --> 00:37:15,980
because previously all we could do would be to access a small portion of the tree.

483
00:37:16,920 --> 00:37:20,260
The U.S. Forest Service Northwest Research Station,

484
00:37:20,261 --> 00:37:24,320
in conjunction with Gifford Pinchot National Forest and the University of Washington,

485
00:37:25,160 --> 00:37:30,380
coordinates as many as 30 research projects at any given time here at the crane.

486
00:37:31,600 --> 00:37:36,220
But the man who deftly places these scientists in the canopy is Mark Creighton.

487
00:37:37,240 --> 00:37:42,100
This crane uses three basic controls, three functions,

488
00:37:42,320 --> 00:37:45,440
and those would be slewing motion, as in a swing,

489
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:49,840
trolley, which allows our basket to go in and out,

490
00:37:49,841 --> 00:37:53,960
and then we also have a hoist which allows the basket to go up and down.

491
00:37:54,660 --> 00:38:02,620
And what we have here is a 3D readout, which is unique to tower cranes for the most part.

492
00:38:03,120 --> 00:38:12,620
It'll give me position, the distance that my basket is out, the distance from the ground it is,

493
00:38:13,160 --> 00:38:19,580
and also in what degrees we are turning so that when we set up a study with the researchers,

494
00:38:19,840 --> 00:38:26,720
They'll go to a location, and we can replicate that location exactly time and time again.

495
00:38:27,520 --> 00:38:30,820
Mark found his dream job when he was hired to operate the research crane,

496
00:38:31,440 --> 00:38:33,920
which is just a few miles from his home.

497
00:38:35,140 --> 00:38:39,540
For this longtime crane operator, there were aspects of the job that took some getting used to.

498
00:38:40,500 --> 00:38:45,240
Everything we do around a construction tower crane is to avoid picking people in a basket.

499
00:38:45,241 --> 00:38:51,420
If we happen to pick personnel in a man basket, we would call that meat on a hook.

500
00:38:52,500 --> 00:38:56,020
And that just lets you know that that's a very important pick.

501
00:38:56,280 --> 00:39:01,940
Over 20 years of operating cranes, I maybe picked a handful of guys in baskets,

502
00:39:02,160 --> 00:39:06,540
and here I've probably picked many, many, many thousands of people.

503
00:39:06,660 --> 00:39:08,300
So it's just opposite ends.

504
00:39:08,940 --> 00:39:13,260
Even as they probe these 220-foot giants for clues to how our planet functions,

505
00:39:14,260 --> 00:39:17,020
These scientists also find time to enjoy the view.

506
00:39:17,600 --> 00:39:21,700
It just gives you a new and interesting perspective on the canopy

507
00:39:21,701 --> 00:39:24,540
to actually be able to look down on it and see the symmetry of the trees.

508
00:39:25,200 --> 00:39:27,340
It's always refreshing.

509
00:39:27,440 --> 00:39:30,100
No matter how many times you go up, it's always refreshing to see that.

510
00:39:31,500 --> 00:39:34,160
Another 220 nautical miles up,

511
00:39:34,161 --> 00:39:39,040
there's an even more spectacular view from the crane that works on the International Space Station.

512
00:39:39,041 --> 00:39:51,851
Launched within the space shuttle in April of 2001, Canadarm2 accomplished its first mission flawlessly with the help of its sister crane, Canadarm1, which rides on

513
00:39:51,852 --> 00:39:54,820
the space shuttle. It deployed itself.

514
00:39:54,821 --> 00:40:01,820
Once the astronauts had unfolded the arm, it stepped off itself from its launch pallet

515
00:40:01,821 --> 00:40:07,920
and attached itself to the side of the space station, and then it let go of the, if you like, the launch pallet,

516
00:40:08,420 --> 00:40:13,640
and then it became an automatic arm, a robotic arm, on the side of the station, which could move around.

517
00:40:14,640 --> 00:40:20,100
Manufactured by MacDonald Detweiler and Associates in Canada, at a cost of nearly $900 million,

518
00:40:20,101 --> 00:40:20,801
dollars.

519
00:40:20,802 --> 00:40:25,560
His vital component of the space station is literally allowing us to reach out even further

520
00:40:25,561 --> 00:40:29,642
into space with its 54-foot-long, 4,000-pound arm.

521
00:40:29,643 --> 00:40:33,480
It is like a video game, but a very complicated

522
00:40:33,481 --> 00:40:36,048
one. We're moving in three dimensions.

523
00:40:36,049 --> 00:40:39,360
The astronauts have to maintain spatial awareness

524
00:40:39,361 --> 00:40:44,095
of exactly where the element is that's being maneuvered towards the station.

525
00:40:44,096 --> 00:40:44,796
Part of the

526
00:40:44,797 --> 00:40:47,800
Canadarm2's task is to construct the growing space station.

527
00:40:48,580 --> 00:40:51,740
It has already helped assemble the United States airlock,

528
00:40:52,160 --> 00:40:55,840
and it continues to add important truss sections to the overall structure,

529
00:40:56,460 --> 00:40:59,900
which is scheduled for completion in 2006.

530
00:41:02,860 --> 00:41:07,740
The crane also makes occasional use of a special dexterous attachment at the end of the arm,

531
00:41:08,520 --> 00:41:09,720
called a human being.

532
00:41:09,900 --> 00:41:14,140
The crew sit on a special fixture on the end of the arm,

533
00:41:14,141 --> 00:41:19,060
and they ride on that to various locations where they can perform their tasks.

534
00:41:19,540 --> 00:41:22,100
And of course, not only do we transport them there,

535
00:41:22,160 --> 00:41:28,460
the platform that's attached to the arm allows the crew, it gives them a work site.

536
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:34,580
Despite the lack of gravity in space, the cranes cannot be made spectacularly long.

537
00:41:35,580 --> 00:41:39,400
Though engineers were able to ignore the static downforce of weight in the arm,

538
00:41:40,200 --> 00:41:41,960
they had to consider its mass.

539
00:41:42,940 --> 00:41:47,280
A massive crane swinging around outside the space station could do a lot of damage.

540
00:41:48,420 --> 00:41:53,320
Further, the fine motor skills required of this dexterous crane would be more difficult to achieve

541
00:41:53,321 --> 00:41:56,280
if the arm was extremely long.

542
00:41:57,120 --> 00:42:02,900
Factors like internal flex and even vibration from the controls are exaggerated the longer the arm.

543
00:42:03,020 --> 00:42:08,320
The Canadarm 2 had to have a full operational life of 15 years or greater

544
00:42:08,321 --> 00:42:11,200
while being in space in all times.

545
00:42:11,760 --> 00:42:15,900
It's not like the Canadarm 1, which is part of the shuttle and comes back to Earth

546
00:42:15,901 --> 00:42:20,280
with every flight and can get repaired and be refurbished and be maintained.

547
00:42:20,560 --> 00:42:25,600
Canadarm 2 is going to remain there for the life of the station and the life of the program.

548
00:42:26,260 --> 00:42:30,440
A special feature of the Canadarm 2 is that both ends are the same

549
00:42:30,441 --> 00:42:37,460
and each can attach to a power data grapple fixture, or PDGF, on the side of the station.

550
00:42:38,320 --> 00:42:42,480
The PDGF links the crane to power, video, and computer data.

551
00:42:43,240 --> 00:42:47,960
The crane moves like an inchworm to each PDGF along the side of the station.

552
00:42:49,200 --> 00:42:54,820
The next technological step for engineers will be to run the crane entirely from Earth,

553
00:42:55,300 --> 00:42:59,920
where operations can be initiated at lower cost and greater margin for safety.

554
00:43:00,700 --> 00:43:02,420
But that poses problems.

555
00:43:02,880 --> 00:43:05,320
There's all sorts of challenges with delays, telemetry.

556
00:43:05,321 --> 00:43:12,500
You have a very great safety concern, of course, because you do have astronauts and a very valuable asset on orbit.

557
00:43:12,960 --> 00:43:17,960
And so we have to make sure that whatever we do is extremely safe, extremely reliable.

558
00:43:18,360 --> 00:43:24,280
The Canadarm 2 is an experiment of sorts, a test to see if practical remote operation is feasible.

559
00:43:25,980 --> 00:43:29,380
After that, it's on to colonization with our trusty cranes.

560
00:43:29,381 --> 00:43:43,381
The challenges that we have met and overcome with the Kandiram 2 will set a very good foundation for building robotic arms for colonization or for activities on

561
00:43:44,123 --> 00:43:44,860
planets.

562
00:43:45,220 --> 00:43:49,500
It's tantalizing to think what challenges engineers will face on other planets.

563
00:43:50,060 --> 00:43:56,140
Once our remote-operated cranes have built, the rudimentary structures will require in order to safely establish colonies.

564
00:43:56,141 --> 00:44:01,140
And once we get there, we'll certainly need even more cranes.

565
00:44:01,141 --> 00:44:05,140
They'll be cousins to the ones we'll continue to use here on Earth.

566
00:44:05,141 --> 00:44:10,140
And they'll be just as indispensable, always ready to give us the lift we need, no matter

567
00:44:10,141 --> 00:44:12,140
what planet we happen to inhabit.

